;;; A `:where` pair inside a pattern is dropped, so the rule fires without
;;; its guard — M1f **W32**, register row `W32/where-in-pattern`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1f.5.0 (2026-11-08) from
;;; S1f.5.11's case (D-15), filed `open` and owned by S1f.5.11, on the user's
;;; ruling of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.28).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `p` facts and a rule whose premise writes `:where (neq ?x a)` inside
;;; the pattern `(p ?x …)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A term takes no keyword, and `:where` is on no form's list (Q-M1e2.28), so
;;; the program is refused at load, with a line naming the rule and the
;;; clause: every command that reads it exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The pair is dropped (Q32), so `r` fires for every `p`: root derives `(q a)`
;;; and `(q b)`, with no line (`ein saturate FILE --dump`), and R is four
;;; states over the candidates `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, exit 0 (`ein solve
;;; FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s,
;;; release and debug alike).

(relation p Node) (relation q Node) (relation hit Node)
(p a) (p b)
(rule r () :match (p ?x :where (neq ?x a)) :assert (q ?x))
